WEBVTT

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.000
Runtime choice affects supported model formats, acceleration backends, memory management,

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:05.806
and tuning controls.

00:00:05.806 --> 00:00:10.279
Llama.cpp offers broad portability and direct control over quantized models.

00:00:10.279 --> 00:00:13.000
Ollama provides a convenient model and service workflow.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.495
LM Studio combines model discovery, local chat, configuration,

00:00:16.495 --> 00:00:18.600
and API serving in a desktop interface.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.765
These options are effective for individual and small-team workloads.

00:00:21.765 --> 00:00:25.800
VLLM targets high-throughput serving on supported hardware and can benefit concurrent

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:29.300
workloads, but its strengths may not matter for one local interactive user.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.500
Open WebUI is a front end rather than the inference engine;

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.825
its responsiveness depends on the backend, network path, and model server beneath it.

00:00:36.825 --> 00:00:40.300
Additional layers are worthwhile only when they solve a real interface or operations

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:43.600
requirement. Match the model format and quantization to the runtime.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.595
A model optimized for one loader may be unsupported or inefficient in another.

00:00:47.595 --> 00:00:50.800
Driver versions, acceleration libraries, runtime builds,

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.300
and hardware architecture form a compatibility chain.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.600
Track known-good versions and verify acceleration after changes.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:00.600
Newer software may improve performance, but it may also change defaults, memory use,

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:01.200
or stability.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.750
Choose the simplest runtime that provides the required model support, control,

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:05.595
and interface.

00:01:05.595 --> 00:01:09.360
Benchmark candidates with the same model and workload, then include setup time,

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.500
reliability, logs, updates, and recovery in the decision.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:16.590
A stable runtime that starts predictably and produces repeatable results is often better

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:19.500
than a more complex stack with a small peak-speed advantage.
